([{"id":"softmax","t":"Stable Softmax","d":"Easy","cat":"NumPy Basics","p":"Vectorization","take":"**Softmax = exp, then normalise. Always subtract the max first**: it changes nothing mathematically and stops `exp` from overflowing.","hint":"Softmax doesn't change if you subtract the same number from every score: the $e^{-c}$ cancels in the fraction. Which number should you subtract so that the biggest exponent is $e^0 = 1$?"},{"id":"cosine-similarity","t":"Cosine Similarity Search","d":"Easy","cat":"Linear Algebra","p":"Dot product","take":"**Cosine similarity = dot product of the two vectors divided by their lengths.** For search, stack the documents in a matrix and do one `M @ query`.","hint":"`a @ b` is the dot product and `np.linalg.norm(a)` is the length. For `most_similar`, `M @ query` gives every row's dot product with the query in one go, and `np.linalg.norm(M, axis=1)` gives every row's length."},{"id":"one-hot-encoding","t":"One-Hot Encoding","d":"Easy","cat":"NumPy Basics","p":"Indexing","take":"**Row `k` of an identity matrix is the one-hot vector for class `k`**; indexing it with the label array encodes everything at once. `argmax(axis=1)` undoes it.","hint":"Think of the identity matrix: row `k` of `np.eye(K)` is the one-hot vector for class `k`. How do you pick several rows at once with an integer array?"},{"id":"standardize-features","t":"Standardize Features","d":"Easy","cat":"NumPy Basics","p":"Axes & broadcasting","take":"**Standardize per column with `axis=0` and let broadcasting do the rest.** Remember to guard against a zero standard deviation, and in a real pipeline compute `mu` and `sd` on the **training** data only.","hint":"`X.mean(axis=0)` gives one mean per column. Subtracting a `(d,)` array from an `(n, d)` array **broadcasts** along the rows. How can you avoid dividing by a std of 0?"},{"id":"matmul-loops-vs-vectorized","t":"Matrix Multiply: Loops vs @","d":"Easy","cat":"Linear Algebra","p":"Matrix multiplication","take":"**Matrix multiplication is dot products of rows with columns; shapes `(m,n)(n,p) -> (m,p)`.** A layer for a whole batch is one `X @ W + b`.","hint":"Entry `(i, j)` of the result is the dot product of **row i of A** with **column j of B**. Three nested loops (i, j, k) do it; `@` does the same in compiled code. For `layer`, remember that `b` needs to be added to every row."},{"id":"power-iteration","t":"Power Iteration for the Top Eigenvector","d":"Medium","cat":"Linear Algebra","p":"Eigenvectors","take":"**Repeated multiplication by a matrix pulls any vector towards its dominant eigenvector.** Normalize every step; the convergence speed depends on the ratio |lambda2| / |lambda1|.","hint":"Multiply by `A`, then divide by the length so the numbers stay bounded. After many rounds the component along the largest eigenvector dominates. The eigenvalue estimate is `v @ A @ v` (the **Rayleigh quotient**, since `v` has length 1)."},{"id":"entropy-cross-entropy-kl","t":"Entropy, Cross-Entropy & KL Divergence","d":"Easy","cat":"Calculus & Probability","p":"Information theory","take":"**Cross-entropy = entropy + KL, and KL is never negative.** With a one-hot `p` it reduces to `-log q[true class]`: the classification loss.","hint":"Select only the positions where `p > 0` with a boolean mask before taking the log. KL is just cross-entropy minus entropy."},{"id":"numerical-gradient-check","t":"Numerical Gradient Check","d":"Medium","cat":"Calculus & Probability","p":"Derivatives","take":"**Always gradient-check hand-written derivatives with central differences.** A relative error around 1e-7 is great; 1e-2 or more means a bug.","hint":"For each coordinate `i`, make a vector `e` that is zero except for `h` at position `i`. Then `(f(x + e) - f(x - e)) / (2 * h)` is that component of the gradient."},{"id":"gaussian-mle","t":"Gaussian Maximum Likelihood","d":"Medium","cat":"Calculus & Probability","p":"MLE","take":"**Gaussian MLE = sample mean and the divide-by-n standard deviation.** The squared-error term of the NLL is why minimising MSE equals maximum likelihood under Gaussian noise.","hint":"Set the derivative of the NLL to zero: the best mean is the average, and the best variance is the average squared deviation from that mean. `np.mean` and `np.std` (with the default `ddof=0`) do exactly this."},{"id":"pairwise-distance-broadcast","t":"Pairwise Euclidean Distances","d":"Medium","cat":"NumPy Basics","p":"Broadcasting","take":"**To compare every row of one array with every row of another, add an axis to each side (`A[:, None]`, `B[None, :]`) and let broadcasting build the pairs.** For big data, use the `A @ B.T` expansion to avoid the 3-D intermediate.","hint":"You want a difference for every pair `(i, j)`. If `A` had shape `(n, 1, d)` and `B` had shape `(1, m, d)`, what would `A - B` look like? Which axis do you sum over?"},{"id":"minmax-zscore-axis","t":"Scale Along an Axis: Min-Max and Z-Score","d":"Easy","cat":"NumPy Basics","p":"Axes & broadcasting","take":"**Pair every reduction with `keepdims=True` when you plan to broadcast the result back.** Then one piece of code serves both `axis=0` (per column, per feature) and `axis=1` (per row).","hint":"Reductions accept `axis` and `keepdims=True`. With `keepdims=True` the statistics have shape `(1, d)` or `(n, 1)`, which broadcasts back against `X` for either axis."},{"id":"moving-average-vectorized","t":"Moving Average Without a Loop","d":"Medium","cat":"NumPy Basics","p":"Vectorization","take":"**A sliding-window sum is a difference of two running totals.** `cumsum` turns an O(n k) loop into O(n) vectorized work. One caveat: with huge numbers the subtraction of large running totals can lose precision.","hint":"Summing each window from scratch repeats work. The running total `cumsum` already contains every window sum: the sum of x[i..i+k-1] is the difference of two running totals. A zero at the front of the cumulative sum makes the indexing tidy."},{"id":"chi-square-independence","t":"Chi-Square Test of Independence","d":"Easy","cat":"Statistics","p":"Counts & contingency tables","take":"**Expected counts are an outer product of the margins.** If the groups behaved identically, each cell would hold its row's share of its column's total. The statistic then measures how far reality is from that picture.","hint":"The whole matrix of expected counts is an **outer product**: row totals (a column vector) times column totals (a row vector), divided by the grand total. `keepdims=True` keeps the shapes right for broadcasting."},{"id":"welch-t-test","t":"Two-Sample Welch t-Test","d":"Medium","cat":"Statistics","p":"Comparing means","take":"**Do not pool the variances.** Welch's test builds the standard error from each group's own `s^2/n` and adjusts the degrees of freedom, so it stays valid with unequal sizes and spreads, and is the safe default.","hint":"Work with the variance **of each mean**, `v = s^2 / n`, for both groups. The standard error is `sqrt(va + vb)` and the degrees of freedom formula is built from the same two numbers. `a.var(ddof=1)` is the sample variance."},{"id":"bootstrap-ci-seeded","t":"Bootstrap Confidence Interval","d":"Medium","cat":"Statistics","p":"Resampling","take":"**Resample with replacement, recompute, take percentiles.** Vectorize by building one `(n_boot, n)` index matrix; a 5-line function replaces a pile of algebra and works for any statistic.","hint":"Make **all** the resamples at once: `rng.integers(0, n, size=(n_boot, n))` is a matrix of indices, `x[idx]` a matrix of resamples (one per row), and `stat(..., axis=1)` gives one statistic per row."},{"id":"benjamini-hochberg-fdr","t":"Benjamini-Hochberg FDR Adjustment","d":"Medium","cat":"Statistics","p":"Multiple testing","take":"**BH compares the i-th smallest p-value with i/m * q.** Adjusted values are the scaled p-values made monotone by a running minimum from the largest rank down; reject everything with an adjusted value at most q.","hint":"Sort, multiply each sorted p by `m / rank`, then take a **running minimum from the largest rank downwards** (`np.minimum.accumulate` on the reversed array) so adjusted values never decrease with p. Finally undo the sort."},{"id":"exact-permutation-test","t":"Exact Permutation Test","d":"Hard","cat":"Statistics","p":"Resampling","take":"**A permutation p-value is just 'how many relabelings look this extreme'.** For tiny samples you can count them all exactly; for larger ones you sample random relabelings instead, using the same logic.","hint":"`itertools.combinations(range(N), n1)` yields every choice of which pooled positions form group 1. Stacking them into an index matrix lets NumPy compute all the group means with one fancy-index and one `sum(axis=1)`. Group 2's sum is the pooled total minus group 1's."},{"id":"linreg-normal-equation","t":"Least Squares with the Normal Equation","d":"Easy","cat":"Regression","p":"Linear regression","take":"**Least squares = solve $X^\\top X w = X^\\top y$.** Add a ones column for the intercept and use `solve`, never an explicit inverse.","hint":"Stack a column of ones in front of `X` with `np.hstack`. Then `np.linalg.solve(A, b)` solves $A\\mathbf w = \\mathbf b$ without ever forming $A^{-1}$."},{"id":"ridge-closed-form","t":"Ridge Regression in Closed Form","d":"Medium","cat":"Regression","p":"Regularization","take":"**Ridge = least squares with α added to the diagonal.** Centre the data so the intercept is not shrunk.","hint":"Centre first: `Xc = X - X.mean(axis=0)`, `yc = y - y.mean()`. Then `np.linalg.solve(Xc.T @ Xc + alpha * np.eye(d), Xc.T @ yc)`. The intercept is `y.mean() - X.mean(axis=0) @ w`."},{"id":"linreg-minibatch-gd","t":"Mini-Batch Gradient Descent for Linear Regression","d":"Medium","cat":"Regression","p":"Gradient descent","take":"**Mini-batch GD = shuffle, slice into batches, one gradient step per batch.** Batch size trades gradient noise against speed.","hint":"Inside the epoch loop: `idx = rng.permutation(n)`, then `for s in range(0, n, batch_size): batch = idx[s:s+batch_size]`. The error is `err = X[batch] @ w + b - y[batch]`."},{"id":"knn-predict","t":"k-Nearest Neighbours Classifier","d":"Easy","cat":"Classification","p":"k-NN","take":"**k-NN = distances, take the k smallest, vote.** Broadcasting computes all distances at once.","hint":"`np.argsort(dist)[:k]` gives the indices of the k smallest distances. `np.bincount(labels)` counts votes, and `np.argmax` of it returns the smallest label on ties. Compute all distances to all training points with broadcasting, no loop needed."},{"id":"logreg-gradient-descent","t":"Logistic Regression with Gradient Descent","d":"Medium","cat":"Classification","p":"Logistic regression","take":"**Logistic regression = sigmoid(linear score), trained by gradient descent; the gradient is Xᵀ(p − y)/n.**","hint":"`p = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(X @ w + b)))`. The gradient is `X.T @ (p - y) / n`: prediction minus truth, just like linear regression."},{"id":"gini-stump","t":"Best Gini Split for a Decision Stump","d":"Hard","cat":"Classification","p":"Decision trees","take":"**Sort once and sweep with cumulative counts**: every split's impurity is O(1), so a whole feature costs O(n log n). That is how real decision-tree libraries find splits.","hint":"For a threshold, count the class-1 rows on each side. With only two classes, Gini is $2p(1-p)$. The efficient way: sort a feature, then use a running (cumulative) count of ones to get the left/right counts for every split position in one pass."},{"id":"kmeans-one-iteration","t":"One k-Means Iteration","d":"Easy","cat":"Clustering & PCA","p":"k-means","take":"**k-means is two lines of linear algebra**: a distance table plus `argmin` (assign), then group means (update). Everything else is repeating it until the labels stop changing.","hint":"Broadcasting gives all the distances at once: `X[:, None, :] - C[None, :, :]` has shape (n, k, d). Sum the squares over the last axis, then `argmin(axis=1)`. Then loop over the k clusters with a boolean mask."},{"id":"kmeans-from-scratch","t":"k-Means from Scratch with Inertia","d":"Medium","cat":"Clustering & PCA","p":"k-means","take":"**k-means never increases the inertia**: assign lowers it, update lowers it, so the loop must stop. But it stops at a *local* minimum, which is why libraries run it from several starts (`n_init`) and use k-means++.","hint":"Reuse the assign / update step from the previous problem inside a loop. After the loop, recompute the distances with the final centroids and sum each point's distance to its own centroid with `d[np.arange(n), labels].sum()`."},{"id":"pca-svd","t":"PCA with SVD and Explained Variance","d":"Medium","cat":"Clustering & PCA","p":"PCA","take":"**PCA is an SVD of the centred data**: right singular vectors are the components, squared singular values (over n−1) are the variances. Components are uncorrelated and sorted by variance.","hint":"`Xc = X - X.mean(axis=0)`. `U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(Xc, full_matrices=False)`. The rows of `Vt` are the components, so `Z = Xc @ Vt[:m].T`. Variances: `s**2 / (n-1)`."},{"id":"confusion-metrics","t":"Confusion Matrix, Precision, Recall & F1","d":"Easy","cat":"Evaluation","p":"Classification metrics","take":"**All the classification metrics are ratios of four counts.** Precision looks down the predicted-positive column, recall along the actual-positive row; F1 punishes a big gap between them.","hint":"Each count is the sum of a boolean AND: `np.sum((p == 1) & (t == 1))` is TP. Divide carefully, with a small helper `safe_div(a, b)` that returns 0.0 when `b == 0`."},{"id":"roc-auc-scores","t":"ROC-AUC from Scores","d":"Medium","cat":"Evaluation","p":"Classification metrics","take":"**AUC is a ranking statistic**: the chance a random positive outscores a random negative. The rank-sum formula computes it in O(n log n) with one sort and no explicit curve.","hint":"Brute force: compare every positive with every negative. Faster: the **rank-sum** (Mann-Whitney) formula. Rank all scores (ties get the average of their ranks, starting at 1); then $\\text{AUC}=\\frac{R_+ - n_+(n_+ + 1)/2}{n_+ n_-}$ where $R_+$ is the sum of the positives' ranks."},{"id":"stratified-kfold","t":"Stratified k-Fold Indices","d":"Medium","cat":"Evaluation","p":"Cross-validation","take":"**Stratification = group by class, then deal round-robin.** Every fold gets the same share of each class (to within one row), so no fold ends up with almost no positives.","hint":"Sort the row indices by class with a **stable** sort (`np.argsort(y, kind=\"stable\")`), so rows within a class stay in order. Then the i-th row in that order goes to fold `i % k`."},{"id":"fisher-lda-direction","t":"Fisher's LDA Direction","d":"Easy","cat":"Classical ML","p":"lda","take":"**Fisher's LDA is one linear solve**: the line between the class means, corrected by the inverse within-class scatter so that noisy directions count less.","hint":"Compute the two class means, centre each class by its own mean, and add the two $\\mathbf X_c^\\top\\mathbf X_c$ products. Then `np.linalg.solve(Sw, m1 - m0)` and divide by the norm."},{"id":"gmm-em-one-iteration","t":"One EM Iteration for a 1-D Gaussian Mixture","d":"Medium","cat":"Classical ML","p":"em","take":"**EM for a mixture is two array lines**: normalise the weighted densities to get responsibilities (E), then take weighted means, variances and shares (M). The log-likelihood can only go up.","hint":"Build an (n, K) matrix of weighted densities with broadcasting: `x[:, None]` against `mu[None, :]`. Row sums are the per-point likelihoods; dividing by them gives the responsibilities. The M-step is column sums of `r`, `r * x`, and `r * (x - new_mu)**2`."},{"id":"viterbi-decoding","t":"Viterbi Decoding for an HMM","d":"Medium","cat":"Classical ML","p":"hmm","take":"**Viterbi is the forward algorithm with max instead of sum, plus backpointers.** It turns an exponential search over K^T paths into T small K×K table lookups.","hint":"Keep `delta[t, j]` = probability of the best path ending in state j at time t and `psi[t, j]` = the predecessor that achieved it. For t ≥ 1, `delta[t-1][:, None] * A` is a K×K table of (from, to) scores; take the max and argmax over `axis=0`."},{"id":"metropolis-hastings-sampler","t":"Metropolis-Hastings Sampler","d":"Hard","cat":"Classical ML","p":"mcmc","take":"**Metropolis in four lines**: propose, compare log-densities, accept with probability min(1, ratio), and **keep the old state when you reject**. Work in logs and cache the current log-density.","hint":"Keep the current log-density in a variable instead of recomputing `logp(x)` every time: it only changes when a proposal is accepted. Compare logs, never exponentiate the ratio."},{"id":"als-user-update","t":"ALS Update for One User","d":"Easy","cat":"Classical ML","p":"recsys-mf","take":"**An ALS step is just a ridge regression** on the rows of the other factor matrix that correspond to observed ratings; every user (and then every item) is solved independently.","hint":"Mask the rows first: `Qo = Q[observed]`, `ro = r[observed]`. Then form the k×k matrix and solve a linear system with `np.linalg.solve` instead of inverting it."},{"id":"adaboost-round","t":"One Round of AdaBoost","d":"Medium","cat":"Classical ML","p":"adaboost","take":"**After an AdaBoost update the learner that was just trained has weighted error exactly 1/2**: the misclassified points together carry half of the weight, so the next learner must do something new.","hint":"`y * pred` is +1 for a correct prediction and -1 for a mistake, so the update factor is `np.exp(-alpha * y * pred)`. The error is `w[pred != y].sum()`."},{"id":"apriori-rules","t":"Frequent Itemsets and Association Rules","d":"Medium","cat":"Classical ML","p":"apriori","take":"**Downward closure drives Apriori**: a frequent itemset has only frequent subsets, so you never count a candidate that has an infrequent part. Rules then come for free from the stored counts.","hint":"Keep a dict for the current level $L_k$. Build candidates by joining pairs from $L_k$ whose union has size k+1, drop any candidate with a k-subset not in $L_k$, then count the survivors. Confidence needs the counts of both A and A∪B, which you already have."},{"id":"kalman-step","t":"One Kalman Filter Step","d":"Medium","cat":"Classical ML","p":"kalman","take":"**Kalman = predict (uncertainty grows), then update (uncertainty shrinks)**, with the gain K = P⁻Hᵀ S⁻¹ deciding how much to trust the measurement versus the prediction.","hint":"The gain solves $\\mathbf K\\mathbf S=\\mathbf P^-\\mathbf H^\\top$. Since `S` is symmetric, `K = np.linalg.solve(S, H @ P_pred.T).T` gives it without an inverse (or `P_pred @ H.T @ inv(S)` in the plain version)."},{"id":"activations-derivatives","t":"Activations and Their Derivatives","d":"Easy","cat":"Neural Net Basics","p":"Activations","take":"**Write each derivative in terms of the forward output** (`f(1-f)`, `1-f²`, a mask): backprop already has `f` stored, so the derivative is nearly free.","hint":"Compute `f` first and reuse it for the derivative: sigmoid' needs only `f * (1 - f)`, tanh' only `1 - f**2`. For a stable sigmoid, only ever exponentiate a **non-positive** number: use `e = np.exp(-np.abs(x))`."},{"id":"dense-layer-forward-backward","t":"A Dense Layer: Forward and Backward","d":"Medium","cat":"Neural Net Basics","p":"Backpropagation","take":"**dX = dout·Wᵀ, dW = Xᵀ·dout, db = Σ dout.** Let the shapes tell you the order of the product: each gradient must have the shape of what it differentiates.","hint":"Think of the shapes. `dW` must be (d_in, d_out): which product of `X` (n, d_in) and `dout` (n, d_out) gives that? And `dX` must be (n, d_in): `dout` times which transpose?"},{"id":"softmax-ce-gradient","t":"Softmax + Cross-Entropy Loss and Gradient","d":"Medium","cat":"Neural Net Basics","p":"Losses","take":"**For softmax + cross-entropy, ∂L/∂logits = (p − onehot) / n.** It is the same 'prediction minus truth' as regression, and each row sums to 0.","hint":"Subtract each row's max before `exp`. Use `log p = z - logsumexp(z)` for the loss. For the gradient, copy `p`, subtract 1 at `[i, y[i]]` with fancy indexing, then divide by n."},{"id":"xor-backprop","t":"Train a 2-Layer Net on XOR with Backprop","d":"Hard","cat":"Neural Net Basics","p":"Backpropagation","take":"**Backprop for a 2-layer net is five lines**: output error `p − y`, `dW2 = A1ᵀ·dz2`, push back with `W2ᵀ`, multiply by the activation slope, `dW1 = Xᵀ·dz1`.","hint":"Backward pass: `dz2 = (p - y)/n`; `dW2 = A1.T @ dz2`; `dA1 = dz2 @ W2.T`; `dz1 = dA1 * (1 - A1**2)` (tanh'); `dW1 = X.T @ dz1`; biases are column sums."},{"id":"tiny-autograd","t":"A Tiny Autograd Value","d":"Hard","cat":"Neural Net Basics","p":"Autograd","take":"**Autograd = record the graph + a local-derivative closure per op, then run the closures in reverse topological order with `+=`.** This is `loss.backward()` in PyTorch.","hint":"Every operation makes a new Value that remembers its parents and a small `_backward` function holding the local derivatives (use `+=`!). For `backward()`, first build a list of all nodes in topological order (parents before children), set the output's grad to 1, then call each node's `_backward` in reverse order."},{"id":"inverted-dropout","t":"Inverted Dropout","d":"Easy","cat":"Training","p":"Regularization","take":"**Inverted dropout multiplies by `mask/(1-p)` in training and does nothing at test time**; the backward pass reuses the same mask.","hint":"The mask and the scaling can be one array: `(rng.random(x.shape) >= p) / (1 - p)`. The forward pass is `x * mask`. What is the derivative of `x * mask` with respect to `x`?"},{"id":"sgd-momentum-adam","t":"SGD with Momentum and Adam Steps","d":"Medium","cat":"Training","p":"Optimizers","take":"**Momentum averages gradients; Adam additionally divides by the running size of the gradient** (and bias-corrects the early steps). On step 1 every parameter moves by about lr.","hint":"Compute the new `m` and `s` first, then the corrected `m_hat`, `s_hat` using `t`, then the step. Momentum is just two lines."},{"id":"batchnorm-forward","t":"Batch Normalization Forward Pass","d":"Medium","cat":"Training","p":"Normalization","take":"**BatchNorm = standardise each feature over the batch, then apply a learned scale and shift;** keep running averages of the statistics for inference time.","hint":"`x.mean(axis=0)` and `x.var(axis=0)` (NumPy's `var` is biased by default) give per-feature statistics for a (n, d) batch. Remember the `n/(n-1)` factor only in the running variance."},{"id":"conv2d-naive","t":"2-D Convolution with Stride and Padding","d":"Medium","cat":"CNNs & RNNs","p":"CNN","take":"**A convolution is a dot product between the kernel and every patch.** Output size: ⌊(n + 2p − k)/s⌋ + 1.","hint":"Pad with `np.pad(x, pad)`. For output cell `(i, j)` the patch is `xp[i*stride : i*stride+kh, j*stride : j*stride+kw]`. To avoid the double loop, `np.lib.stride_tricks.sliding_window_view(xp, (kh, kw))` gives every patch at once (shape `(H', W', kh, kw)`); slice `[::stride, ::stride]` for the stride."},{"id":"max-pool-2d","t":"Max Pooling","d":"Easy","cat":"CNNs & RNNs","p":"CNN","take":"**Pooling is a window reduction with no weights**: the same code with `.mean` gives average pooling.","hint":"`sliding_window_view(x, (size, size), axis=(-2, -1))` gives every window; keep every `stride`-th one in both directions and take the maximum over the last two axes."},{"id":"conv-im2col","t":"Convolution as a Matrix Multiply (im2col)","d":"Hard","cat":"CNNs & RNNs","p":"CNN","take":"**im2col turns convolution into a single matrix multiplication.** It costs extra memory (patches overlap) but makes use of the fastest operation on any CPU or GPU.","hint":"`sliding_window_view(xp, (kh, kw), axis=(1, 2))` has shape `(C, H', W', kh, kw)`. Slice the stride, then move the axes so that `(C, kh, kw)` come first and `(oh, ow)` last, and `reshape` to `(C*kh*kw, oh*ow)`. The filters are `W.reshape(F, -1)`."},{"id":"rnn-forward","t":"RNN Cell Forward Pass over a Sequence","d":"Medium","cat":"CNNs & RNNs","p":"RNN","take":"**An RNN is a for-loop that reuses the same weights at every step and passes the hidden state along.**","hint":"The time loop cannot be removed (each step needs the previous state), but inside each step use matrix-vector products: `Wx @ x + Wh @ h + b`."},{"id":"lstm-step","t":"LSTM Cell Step","d":"Hard","cat":"CNNs & RNNs","p":"RNN","take":"**An LSTM step is one stacked matrix multiply followed by four element-wise gate operations**: forget the old, write the new, reveal part of the result.","hint":"Compute `z` with ONE matrix multiplication and slice `z[0:H]`, `z[H:2H]`, `z[2H:3H]`, `z[3H:4H]`. A safe sigmoid is `0.5 * (1 + np.tanh(z / 2))`."},{"id":"bpe-merge-step","t":"One BPE Merge Step","d":"Easy","cat":"Tokenization","p":"BPE","take":"**BPE is just: count neighbouring pairs, glue the winner, repeat.** The list of winners, in order, is the tokenizer.","hint":"Count with `collections.Counter` over `zip(symbols, symbols[1:])`. Then walk through each word with an index `i`: if `symbols[i], symbols[i+1]` equal the pair, append the glued symbol and jump by 2, otherwise append one symbol and jump by 1."},{"id":"bpe-tokenizer","t":"Train a Tiny BPE Tokenizer","d":"Medium","cat":"Tokenization","p":"BPE","take":"**A BPE tokenizer is its ordered merge list.** Training learns the list, encoding replays it.","hint":"Reuse the idea of one merge step in a loop: count pairs, take the best, rewrite the words, append the pair to `merges`. Encoding replays the same rewrite for each learned pair, in order."},{"id":"scaled-dot-product-attention","t":"Scaled Dot-Product Attention","d":"Easy","cat":"Attention","p":"Attention","take":"**softmax(QKᵀ/√d)·V: two matrix products with a row-wise softmax in between.**","hint":"`Q @ K.T` gives all scores at once. Subtract each row's maximum before `np.exp`, divide by the row sum with `keepdims=True`."},{"id":"causal-mask-softmax","t":"Causal Masked Softmax","d":"Medium","cat":"Attention","p":"Attention","take":"**Mask the scores with -inf first, then softmax.** The surviving weights still sum to 1.","hint":"Build a boolean matrix of forbidden pairs: `np.triu(np.ones((T, T), bool), 1)` for the future, and `np.arange(T) >= length` (broadcast over rows) for padding. `np.where(forbidden, -np.inf, scores)` then softmax; `exp(-inf)` is 0."},{"id":"multi-head-split-merge","t":"Multi-Head Split and Merge","d":"Medium","cat":"Attention","p":"Attention","take":"**Reshape to (T, h, d_k) first, then transpose.** Going straight to (h, T, d_k) scrambles tokens and heads.","hint":"Reshape to `(T, h, d_k)` first (this keeps each row's columns grouped by head), and only then `transpose(1, 0, 2)`. Merging is the same two steps in reverse."},{"id":"layernorm-forward","t":"LayerNorm Forward","d":"Easy","cat":"Transformer Blocks","p":"LayerNorm","take":"**LayerNorm normalises each token's own feature vector, not across the batch.**","hint":"`x.mean(axis=-1, keepdims=True)` and `x.var(axis=-1, keepdims=True)`; `keepdims` makes the shapes broadcast against `x`."},{"id":"sinusoidal-positional-encoding","t":"Sinusoidal Positional Encoding","d":"Medium","cat":"Transformer Blocks","p":"Positions","take":"**Each pair of dimensions is a clock hand turning at its own speed; together they give every position a unique pattern.**","hint":"Make `pos` a column `(T, 1)` and `i` a row `(1, d/2)` holding 0, 2, 4, ... Then `angle = pos / 10000 ** (i / d)` is `(T, d/2)`. Fill `pe[:, 0::2]` with sin and `pe[:, 1::2]` with cos."},{"id":"pre-ln-block","t":"A Pre-LN Transformer Block","d":"Hard","cat":"Transformer Blocks","p":"Transformer block","take":"**Pre-LN block = two residual updates, each reading a LayerNorm'd copy: x + Attn(LN(x)), then x + MLP(LN(x)).**","hint":"Structure it as: `a = LN(x)`; project to Q, K, V and split heads; causal-masked softmax attention; merge heads, `@ Wo`; `x = x + att`; then the MLP on `LN(x)`; `x = x + mlp`. Remember to add to the **original** `x`, not the normalised copy."},{"id":"bigram-perplexity","t":"Bigram Language Model Perplexity","d":"Easy","cat":"Decoding & Efficiency","p":"Language models","take":"**Perplexity = exp(average negative log-likelihood per token).** Zero counts give infinite perplexity unless you smooth.","hint":"Count pairs with `np.add.at(counts, (a, b), 1)`. The denominator of row `a` is `counts[a].sum() + alpha * V`; guard it against 0 (only possible when `alpha = 0`). Look up all test pairs at once with fancy indexing."},{"id":"int8-quantize","t":"Symmetric int8 Quantize / Dequantize","d":"Easy","cat":"Decoding & Efficiency","p":"Quantization","take":"**q = round(w / scale), w_hat = q * scale, with scale = max|w| / 127.** The error per weight is at most scale / 2.","hint":"`np.abs(W).max(axis=1, keepdims=True) / 127` gives one scale per row. Replace zero scales with 1 using `np.where`. Then `np.round(W / scale)`, `np.clip(..., -127, 127)`, `.astype(np.int8)`."},{"id":"lora-forward-params","t":"LoRA Forward Pass and Parameter Count","d":"Medium","cat":"Decoding & Efficiency","p":"Fine-tuning","take":"**LoRA adds (alpha/r) · B(Ax) to the frozen Wx and trains only A and B: r(d_in + d_out) numbers instead of d_in · d_out.** With B = 0 the layer starts identical to the base model.","hint":"`x @ W.T` is the frozen path; `(x @ A.T) @ B.T` is the adapter path (shapes `(n, r)` then `(n, d_out)`). Multiply the adapter path by `alpha / r`. The parameter count is `r * (d_in + d_out)`."},{"id":"sampling-filters","t":"Temperature, Top-k and Top-p Filtering","d":"Medium","cat":"Decoding & Efficiency","p":"Sampling","take":"**Order is: scale by temperature, softmax, filter (top-k, then top-p), renormalise, sample.** Top-p adapts the number of kept tokens to how confident the model is.","hint":"Sort token ids with `np.argsort(-p, kind='stable')`. For top-k, zero everything in `order[top_k:]`. For top-p, `csum = np.cumsum(p[order])`, keep `np.searchsorted(csum, top_p - 1e-12) + 1` tokens. Renormalise after each filter."},{"id":"beam-search-toy","t":"Greedy vs Beam Search","d":"Medium","cat":"Decoding & Efficiency","p":"Decoding","take":"**Beam search keeps the best `width` sequences by cumulative log-probability at every step; greedy is `width = 1`.** It can find sequences greedy misses, at `width` times the cost.","hint":"Keep a list of `(sequence, logprob)` beams. For the first step use `start`; later steps use `trans[sequence[-1]]`. In NumPy: `scores = lp[:, None] + np.log(row_probs)` has shape `(beams, V)`; flatten it, take `np.argsort(-flat, kind='stable')[:width]`, then `parent = idx // V`, `token = idx % V`."},{"id":"kv-cache-attention","t":"Incremental Attention with a KV Cache","d":"Hard","cat":"Decoding & Efficiency","p":"Inference","take":"**With a KV cache each step projects one token and reads the stored keys and values; the output is identical to full causal attention.** The cache trades memory for compute.","hint":"Keep two Python lists `k_cache` and `v_cache`, or preallocate `(T, d)` arrays and fill row `t`. At step `t`, `scores = K[:t+1] @ q / sqrt(d)`; subtract the max before `exp`; output `weights @ V[:t+1]`."},{"id":"rag-chunk-overlap","t":"Overlapping Word Chunks","d":"Easy","cat":"RAG","p":"Chunking","take":"**Chunk starts form an arithmetic progression with step `size - overlap`**; overlap multiplies the index size by about `size / step`.","hint":"The step between chunk starts is `size - overlap`. Either loop with a `while` and break once `start + size >= n`, or compute all starts at once with `range(0, max(1, n - overlap), step)`."},{"id":"tfidf-retrieval","t":"TF-IDF Retrieval with Cosine Similarity","d":"Medium","cat":"RAG","p":"Retrieval","take":"**Retrieval = unit-length vectors plus one matrix-vector product.** Normalising up front turns cosine similarity into a plain dot product, for TF-IDF and for neural embeddings alike.","hint":"Build a documents-by-vocabulary count matrix, multiply by the idf vector, normalise the rows. Embed the query the same way. All scores are then one matrix-vector product. `np.argsort(-scores, kind='stable')` gives the tie rule for free."},{"id":"bm25-scoring","t":"BM25 Scoring","d":"Medium","cat":"RAG","p":"Retrieval","take":"**BM25 = idf times a saturating, length-normalised term frequency.** It is the strong keyword baseline that hybrid search combines with vectors.","hint":"Precompute lengths, avgdl and document frequencies once. For each query word, add `idf * f*(k1+1) / (f + k1*(1 - b + b*len/avgdl))` to every document's score; with NumPy this is a vector over documents."},{"id":"reciprocal-rank-fusion","t":"Reciprocal Rank Fusion","d":"Easy","cat":"RAG","p":"Hybrid search","take":"**RRF merges rankings using only ranks, never raw scores**, so BM25 and cosine scores on different scales can be combined without tuning weights.","hint":"Accumulate into a dict while enumerating each list with `start=1`. Sort with the key `(-score, id)`. Round only when producing the output, so the tie-break compares exact values."},{"id":"retrieval-recall-mrr","t":"Recall@k and MRR","d":"Easy","cat":"RAG","p":"Retrieval evaluation","take":"**Recall@k asks \"is it in the top k at all?\"; MRR asks \"how high is the first correct result?\"** Use recall@k to tune how many chunks go in the prompt and MRR to judge the ordering.","hint":"Build a Q x L boolean matrix `hit[q, j]` = whether `retrieved[q][j]` is relevant. Recall is `hit[:, :k].sum(1) / sizes`. The first hit position is `hit.argmax(1)`, valid only if the row has any hit."},{"id":"validate-tool-call","t":"Validate a Tool Call Against Its Schema","d":"Easy","cat":"Agents","p":"Tool use","take":"**Validate model-made arguments like untrusted input, and return messages the model can act on**: it will use them to fix its next call.","hint":"Make a table from schema type names to Python types, and write one helper that says whether a value has the right type (excluding bool for integer and number). Then add errors to a list in the order the rules give."},{"id":"trajectory-scoring","t":"Score an Agent Trajectory","d":"Medium","cat":"Agents","p":"Evaluation","take":"**Compare trajectories as multisets for \"what was done\" and as subsequences for \"in which order\"**; extra calls show up as lost precision.","hint":"Turn each call into a hashable key such as `(name, json.dumps(args, sort_keys=True))`. `Counter(a) & Counter(b)` is the multiset intersection. For the order, walk `actual` once with a pointer into `expected`."},{"id":"memory-token-budget","t":"Trim Messages to a Token Budget","d":"Medium","cat":"Agents","p":"Memory","take":"**Trim by whole units from the oldest end, and always pin the system prompt**: a tool result without its call (or a hole in the middle) confuses the model more than a shorter history does.","hint":"Group the non-system messages into units first (an assistant message plus the tool messages that follow it, or a single message). Then walk the units from the newest to the oldest, adding each while the total stays within the budget, and stop at the first unit that does not fit."},{"id":"tool-router-errors","t":"A Tool Router with Error Handling","d":"Medium","cat":"Agents","p":"Tool use","take":"**Never let a tool call crash the loop: validate the call, catch the tool's errors, and return a short message the model can act on.**","hint":"Check the name first. Use `inspect.signature(fn).bind(**args)` inside a try to find argument problems **before** calling, so a TypeError raised inside the tool is not mistaken for a bad call. Then call the function in a try/except Exception."},{"id":"react-loop-fake-llm","t":"A ReAct Loop with a Scripted LLM","d":"Medium","cat":"Agents","p":"Agent loop","take":"**An agent loop is: call the model, parse its reply, run the tool, append the observation, repeat; errors are observations, and the step limit is the only thing that guarantees it ends.**","hint":"Keep the transcript as one growing string. Parse with the regex from the statement, then `json.loads` the arguments inside a try, then call the tool inside a try. Build the observation text in one place."},{"id":"retry-backoff-jitter","t":"Retry with Exponential Backoff and Jitter","d":"Hard","cat":"Agents","p":"Reliability","take":"**Retry only errors that can go away, wait exponentially longer each time with random jitter, cap the wait and the attempts, and make the tool idempotent so a retry is safe.**","hint":"A for loop over attempts. In the except branch, if this was the last attempt raise; otherwise compute the bound `min(cap, base * 2 ** (n - 1))`, draw one uniform number, and sleep."}]).forEach((p) => PRAC.push(p));
